Before You Call a Workbench Task Done

**Status:** draft requiring manual review before recording or publication  
**Product lane:** ProChat Workbench  
**Bounded outcome:** verify one completed task through scope, validation, diff, staged state, and limitations

Today I’m going to show you the checklist I use before calling a ProChat Workbench task done.

Not when the answer sounds confident.

Not when the file looks improved.

When the evidence supports completion.

The result is one reviewed task with a verified scope and an explicit Git state.

My name is Steve.

I spent more than 12 years testing software.

A completed change needs more than activity.

It needs evidence that the requested outcome happened without crossing the approved boundary.

For this demonstration, the task is already implemented in a sanitized repository.

One documentation file was supposed to change.

No other task file was approved.

Nothing was supposed to be staged or committed yet.

Let’s start with the final result.

The changed-path list contains the approved file and one unrelated pre-existing file.

The task diff contains only the intended wording change.

The targeted documentation check passes.

The staged-path list is empty.

That is the result we are going to verify step by step.

Check one.

Confirm the source.

I verify that Workbench is still operating on the repository selected for this task.

A correct file path in the wrong source is still the wrong context.

Check two.

Restate the approved scope.

One exact documentation path may change.

The unrelated pre-existing file must remain untouched.

No staging.

No commit.

This gives the review a concrete boundary.

Check three.

List every changed path.

I compare the current Git status with the before-state captured at the start.

The unrelated file is still present exactly as unrelated work.

The approved task file is the only new change.

No unexpected path appears.

Check four.

Review the task diff.

I look at the exact added and removed lines.

The wording matches the requested outcome.

The surrounding section remains intact.

There is no accidental rewrite.

There is no hidden cleanup.

Check five.

Run the smallest meaningful validation.

For this task, that is a documentation check covering the changed rule.

The check passes.

That result proves the document satisfies that check.

It does not prove every statement is factually correct.

It does not prove unrelated parts of the repository work.

Check six.

Review the staged state.

I list the staged paths without limiting the check to one folder.

The list is empty.

That matches the task instruction.

If the task had approved staging, I would expect an exact path list instead.

Check seven.

State the limitation.

This was a bounded documentation task.

The validation does not cover application behavior, security, performance, or future compatibility.

Those areas were outside scope.

Now I compare all seven checks.

The selected source is correct.

The approved path changed.

The unrelated file remains untouched.

The diff matches the request.

The targeted check passes.

Nothing is staged.

The limitation is explicit.

Those pieces agree.

Now I can call the task complete.

The control point is simple.

Completion is a set of verified states.

It is not a feeling.

It is not the amount of work performed.

And it is not a confident final message from an assistant.

The next action is simple.

Before finishing your next guarded task, inspect scope, validation, diff, staged state, and limitations separately.

Do not let one passing check stand in for all of them.

Before recording or publishing this script, the source, diff, validation output, staged-path list, and limitation statement must be reviewed manually.
